 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Average output current from PWM data",
   "expr": "IOUT = (IMAX / 256) × Σ(n=0..7) D[n] × 2^n",
   "where": {
    "IOUT": "average current of one channel",
    "IMAX": "full-scale channel current set by REXT",
    "D[n]": "bit n of that channel's PWM register (0 or 1)"
   },
   "note": "Worked case: PWM = 1011 0101 gives IOUT = IMAX × (2^0 + 2^2 + 2^4 + 2^5 + 2^7) / 256.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Full-scale output current",
   "expr": "IMAX = x × VEXT / REXT",
   "where": {
    "x": "58.5",
    "VEXT": "R_EXT pin voltage, 1.3 V",
    "REXT": "resistor from R_EXT to GND, 2 kΩ minimum recommended"
   },
   "note": "Stated for VOUT = 0.8 V. REXT = 3.3 kΩ gives about 23 mA; 2 kΩ gives 38 mA.",
   "page": 10
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Channels and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Eighteen constant-current sinks, OUT1 to OUT18, share one full-scale current IMAX chosen by the resistor on R_EXT, up to 38 mA per channel. Each channel then has its own 8-bit PWM duty value, giving 256 average-current levels, and its own on/off bit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current reference",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The R_EXT block sets a reference current IS = 7.8 / REXT, which a switched current mirror copies to OUT1 to OUT18; a comparator between each PWM register and a counter clocked by the internal oscillator gates the mirror. The chip also holds a power-on reset and a bandgap reference."
  },
  {
   "topic": "I2C bus",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Control is over a two-wire I2C bus at up to 400 kHz. The 8-bit slave address is fixed at 1010 1000; the part accepts writes only. SDA is bidirectional and open-collector and needs a pull-up (4.7 kΩ is typical); SCL is an input only. Data is sampled while SCL is high and SDA must be idle high between transfers."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Write sequence",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A write is: START (SDA falls while SCL is high), the address byte MSB first, an acknowledge from the chip (it pulls SDA low for the ninth clock), the register address byte MSB first and another acknowledge, the data byte MSB first and a third acknowledge, then STOP (SDA rises while SCL is high). If the address is not acknowledged, the master should issue STOP and give up the transfer."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Address auto increment",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "After each data byte is acknowledged the internal register pointer advances by one, so several consecutive registers can be loaded in one transfer: send the first register's address, then the data bytes back to back, then STOP."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Update register",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Writes to the PWM registers (01h to 12h) and LED Control registers (13h to 15h) land in holding registers and do not change the outputs until 0000 0000 is written to the Update Register, 16h."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Writing 0000 0000 to the Reset Register, 17h, returns all registers to their defaults. Power-up does the same, so the outputs start off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "PWM and breathing",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The PWM register value is the step number out of 256 (0000 0100 is step four). Rewriting the PWM registers in a steady stream makes LEDs fade up and down. Stepping through a gamma table instead of linear values makes the fade look even to the eye at the cost of fewer distinct levels: 32 steps suit a 1 s breathing cycle and 64 steps a 2 s cycle, and the final count depends on the LED and how the product looks."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "In either shutdown mode every register keeps its contents, so shutdown can save power or, entered and left repeatedly, flash the display. Software shutdown: clear SSD (bit D0 of register 00h); all current sources turn off and supply current falls to 3.1 µA typical. Hardware shutdown: drive SDB low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Place the IC far from a mobile phone's antenna to avoid EMI. On the QFN-24, connect the thermal pad to GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package variation",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The QFN-24 thermal pad and the SOP-24 body outline differ in shape between assembly factories. The SOP-24 outline follows JEDEC MS-013, with millimetres the controlling dimension."
  }
 ],
